window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][128] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Launch a mobile wallet in a market where card use is low. (Use India as the worked case.)</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build a wallet where users can store money and pay at shops,&quot; with no analysis of what people use instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tests the premise by asking what people use in place of cards, identifies the rival, chooses a segment and designs around regulation and trust.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that a free interoperable rail changes what a wallet can offer, competes on what a device maker can uniquely provide (security, setup, integration across devices, privacy) and not on a new balance, and handles revenue, regulation and distribution.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll test the premise first: &quot;card use is low&quot; does not mean people lack ways to pay. It means the way they pay is something else, and the answer depends on what. In India that is largely interoperable account-to-account transfers through the Unified Payments Interface (UPI), scanned from a QR code or approved in a payment app <code>Public</code> <code>[V: reference summaries of NPCI]</code>. Many different apps connect to the same rail, and merchant payments from bank accounts have carried no merchant fee by policy since 2020, with a 0.4% merchant discount rate (capped at ₹300) on merchant payments above ₹2,000 from 15 October 2026 <code>[V: merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026, as reproduced by FinTech BizNews]</code>. So the rival is not cards; it is free, interoperable and already habitual. A new wallet with a stored balance of its own has to beat that, and it has no natural advantage. <em>Goal and company.</em> I'll assume a device maker, so the setting is a phone, a watch and the services around them, and the goal is that the device becomes the way people pay, which strengthens the whole ecosystem. I know this only from public information, and nothing here describes any existing product. <em>Segment.</em> By behavior: urban smartphone owners who pay in shops several times a day; people who have a bank account but no card; people new to digital payments in smaller towns. I choose the first, because the frequency is high, they have capable devices and the habit is established, so the switching question is clear. I give up first-time digital payers, who need a different, assisted design, and I'd treat them as a later segment. <em>Constraint stack.</em> Payments bind: the dominant rail decides everything. Trust follows: people entrust money to an app, so the app's identity, security and dispute route matter. Regulation sets the order of work: payment intermediaries need authorization, and payment data must be stored in India (the Reserve Bank of India's 2018 directive) <code>Public</code> <code>[V: RBI FAQ, 2019; RBI payment aggregator directions, 2025]</code>, and general data-protection rules apply (the DPDP Act, in phases) <code>[V: law-firm summaries of the DPDP Rules]</code>. <em>Options.</em> | Option | Idea | Assessment | |---|---|---| | A. A closed wallet with its own balance | Users load money; pays within the wallet | Competes with free interoperable apps; needs licensing; no reason to switch | | B. A front end to the interoperable rail | The wallet lets users pay from their bank accounts through the rail, with the device's strengths | Fits the market; differentiates on trust, ease and security | | C. A pass wallet for tickets, transit and loyalty cards | Not payments; passes and identity | Lower regulatory burden; a smaller first step | | D. Stay out of payments | Focus on identity and passes | Safe and passive | <em>What a device maker adds.</em> On-device authentication (a fingerprint or face check), secure storage of credentials, a quick setup that finds the user's existing bank account, a tap or a scan that works the same on a phone and a watch, and a privacy stance (what stays on the device and what the company never sees). These are reasons to prefer the device's wallet that do not require a new balance. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend B with C: a wallet that connects to the interoperable rail through an authorized app provider and a partnership with banks, plus passes for transit and tickets as a second use. It gives up the control of a closed wallet and a revenue stream from float, and gains fit with how people pay. Revenue is indirect (a stronger ecosystem) and perhaps from services, since a fee per payment is limited on this rail (none up to 2,000 rupees; 0.4% above that from 15 October 2026) <code>[V: merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026, as reproduced by FinTech BizNews]</code>. Distribution: bank partnerships and merchant acceptance through QR codes that merchants already display. Next I'd add the watch and assisted setup for first-time users. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of eligible users who complete setup and make a first payment within seven days. Input: the share of setups that succeed on the first try. Guardrails: payment success rate, fraud losses and time to resolve a dispute. Counter metric: users who set the wallet up and go back to their previous payment app within a month, which shows whether it adds anything. <em>Risks and limits.</em> Regulation could delay the start, so authorization and data storage begin first. Trust: a failed payment with no clear dispute route loses a user for good. I would not launch without a dispute route and clear refund rules. I'd change my plan if research showed that the habit was so strong, and the free apps so good, that setup alone would not move people; then C becomes the product. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple publishes a privacy position and designs hardware and software together, and <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that wallet and payments prompts test local-rail understanding and privacy <code>[V: Apple privacy pages and integrated hardware, software and services; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any existing Apple product or its availability in any country.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a short-term credit feature for delivery workers whose income is irregular. How do you avoid harming them?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A quick loan with a clear interest rate,&quot; with no analysis of harm.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the real need, designs a product that fits irregular income, and names the risk of a debt cycle with design responses.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sets a written limit and a stop rule, measures repeat borrowing as a harm signal, protects data and consent, and notes the conflict of interest in a platform lending to its own workers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll start with the check (FW-C09-07), because for credit the harm question shapes the product. The user is described by what products lack for them: delivery workers who are paid per job, whose earnings vary week to week while costs (fuel, repairs, a phone bill) come on fixed dates. The need is bridging a gap of a few days. Existing options are informal lenders or loans with high fees and fixed monthly repayments that do not fit irregular income. <em>Design.</em> An advance against earnings already scheduled to arrive, repaid automatically from those earnings, with a flat, stated fee and no compounding interest. A cap: an advance may be at most a set share of average weekly earnings (say 30%, invented). No rollover: an advance cannot be extended or refinanced, and a new one cannot be taken until the last is repaid. The price is shown in the local language before the person agrees, as a single figure and in the effective annual cost, with a clear way to say no. Support is by voice as well as text. <em>Harm signals.</em> The main risk is a debt cycle: a worker takes an advance every week because the last one left a gap. I'd measure the repeat-advance rate, the share of users who take a new advance within seven days of repaying, and treat a high rate as a sign that the product is creating dependence, not bridging a gap. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of users who repay on time and do not take another advance within seven days. Guardrails: the effective cost of the fee, complaints, and the share of earnings taken by repayment. Counter metrics: the repeat-advance rate and the share of users who use it every week. Stop rule: if more than 25% of users take an advance in each of eight consecutive weeks (invented), I'd stop growth and redesign, since that pattern signals dependence. <em>Conflict of interest and data.</em> A platform that employs the worker and lends to the worker has more power than an ordinary lender. Repayment from earnings makes repayment safe for the company and can leave the worker with too little, so the repayment share is capped. The platform's earnings data is used to decide the limit, and the worker must understand and agree to that use; the data is not sold or used for other purposes. <em>Regulation.</em> Lending usually needs authorization and consumer-protection compliance, and details vary by country <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>, so I'd involve legal teams first or partner with a licensed lender. <em>My limit.</em> I would not launch credit with fees that trap people in repeated borrowing, with hidden or rolling charges, or with collection methods that shame or threaten, even if that version grew fastest. I'd change my plan if the first cohort showed that most advances were repaid and not repeated; I'd widen the cap carefully, and if the opposite, I'd not launch at scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your product succeeded in its home market and failed in the first country it entered. You have two weeks to write a post-mortem. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Conclude that the market was not ready and list lessons.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates assumptions from evidence, finds which dimension broke, uses funnel data and interviews, and proposes a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Compares the new market's funnel step by step with the home one, includes people who left, tests alternative explanations, sets criteria for another attempt or an exit, and avoids blame.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a diagnosis with a decision at the end, and I'd refuse the conclusion &quot;the market was not ready&quot; until the evidence earns it. The product has a funnel in both markets, so the first move is to compare them. <em>Week 1: evidence.</em> Day 1 and 2: funnel by step for the new market and the home market, split by device, connection and channel, to find where the new market loses people (acquisition, sign-up, first use, payment, return). Day 2 to 4: the six dimensions as a checklist of what we assumed against what was true: users, infrastructure, regulation, competition, distribution and price. Day 3 to 5: interviews with ten people who installed and left, and five who stayed; calls with the local team and any partners; support tickets by theme. I'd include the people who left, because the success stories are not where the answer is. <em>Example of what the evidence might show (invented).</em> | Assumption | Evidence | Verdict | |---|---|---| | Users have a personal phone | 8 of 12 interviewees shared a phone | Wrong; shared use | | Card payment works | 60% of payment attempts failed or were abandoned at the card step | Wrong; payments | | Paid ads will bring users at home-market cost | Cost per install was three times higher and retention was lower | Wrong; distribution | | Translation is enough | Support tickets about language were 5% of the total | Mostly right | <em>Alternative explanations.</em> A single cause is unusual. I'd ask whether launch timing, a competitor's move, or a technical fault (a payment provider outage) explains part of it, and whether the home market's success depended on conditions that do not exist here (for example, a network effect that needs density). <em>Week 2: synthesis and decision.</em> A short decision memo (FW-C02-06): what happened, which assumptions failed and what the evidence is, what we do not know, the options, and a recommendation. Options: iterate in the same market with specific fixes, narrow to one city or segment, change the model (a partner for distribution or a local payment method), or exit. For each, I'd write the condition that would make it right, such as &quot;continue only if payment success reaches 85% and week-four retention reaches 20% within a quarter (invented).&quot; <em>Blame and learning.</em> I'd write the post-mortem about decisions and information, not about people, and I'd hold a pre-mortem (FW-C02-05) for any second attempt: assume it failed in a year and ask why. I'd also record what worked, since a failed entry usually contains a part that did. I'd change my recommendation if the funnel showed that most of the loss happened before the product was even seen, in which case the question is distribution and not the product.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> We have a designer. What is the PM's role in UX?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The PM decides what to build and the designer makes it look nice.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Splits ownership of the problem and the solution, and names what the PM contributes.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives examples of problem framing versus solution dictating, mentions research, tradeoffs and decisions, and says how disagreements are settled.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd say the PM owns the problem and the outcome, and the designer owns the craft of the solution. My job is to make the problem clear: who the user is, what they are trying to do, what success looks like, what constraints we have (time, platform, technology, policy), and what we have learned from research and data. The designer's job is to turn that into a flow and an interface that works and feels right. The engineer tells us what can be built well. The difference shows in how I give input. &quot;Make the button blue and move it to the top&quot; hands the designer a solution and removes their craft. &quot;Forty percent of first-time users stall at the password step; I want 70% to finish, and we cannot add a screen&quot; gives them a problem and criteria, and they may find a better answer than I would have, such as showing the rules as people type. My other contributions: attending research sessions so that I see users struggle first-hand, making the tradeoffs and calls on scope, checking designs against the problem and not against taste, and bringing engineers in early. When we disagree, I'd propose a quick test with five users and let evidence decide before rank does. I'd keep the decision on what to build and the success criteria, and I would defer to the designer on how it should look and work unless it fails the problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A checkout screen shows &quot;Error 4021&quot; when a payment fails. What is wrong, and what do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The message is ugly; make it friendlier.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the heuristic (help with errors), the cost to the user, and a better message.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Looks at the whole state (recovery, preserved data, support, tracking), prioritizes by impact and says how to measure the fix.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The principle is help with errors: an error should say, in plain words, what went wrong and what the person can do. &quot;Error 4021&quot; tells the user nothing, so I'd expect them to retry blindly, abandon, or contact support. At checkout, the cost is a lost order and, at a payment, a loss of trust: people worry whether they were charged. <em>A better message and the state around it.</em> &quot;Your card was declined. Check the number and expiry, try another card, or use a different payment method. You have not been charged.&quot; Plus: keep everything they typed and the cart intact, put the cursor at the field most likely wrong, offer alternative payment methods as buttons, and show a way to get help. The code can stay as a small reference for support. <em>Different failures need different messages.</em> A declined card, a network failure and a provider outage have different fixes. A single message for all of them is a smaller version of the same problem, so I'd ask engineering to map the codes to the main cases. <em>Priority and measurement.</em> This is a high-severity state because it sits at the revenue moment. I'd measure the share of failed payments that lead to a completed purchase within the session (recovery rate) and the support contacts about payment, and test the new message with five users shown a declined card. I'd also check accessibility: the message is announced by screen readers and does not rely on color alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> The deadline is in two weeks and the UX is rough. Do you ship?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ship, we can fix it later,&quot; or &quot;No, quality comes first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets a quality bar by layer and by risk, and decides what to cut.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates accessibility, safety and honest information (not negotiable) from polish, records accepted rough edges with a date, and sets a measure and a rollback.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd decide by risk, using the quality dial (FW-C10-05): works, usable, delightful. I'd ask three things: what is the cost if a user fails here, how many users are affected, and what do we learn by shipping now? <em>Not negotiable.</em> The feature must work (correct, reliable), the core path must be usable, and accessibility basics (labels, contrast, targets) and honest information (clear pricing, clear errors) must be in place. These are not polish. States matter too: the empty and error states are where users get stuck, so they get at least a plain message and a next step. <em>Negotiable.</em> Animations and micro-interactions, secondary screens, visual refinement, edge-case flows that few people reach. I'd cut those first. <em>Make it explicit.</em> I'd write a short list of accepted rough edges with an owner and a date to fix them, since polish debt grows if no one tracks it. I'd release in stages (a small share of users first) with a guardrail on task completion and support contacts and a rollback ready, so a rough UX reaches few people before we learn how rough. <em>Decision.</em> &quot;Ship the core path with accessibility and states intact in two weeks to 10% of users; defer the animations and the settings polish to the next sprint, with a date.&quot; What would change my mind: if the rough area is a trust moment (payment, privacy) or the first-run experience, I'd hold the date, because failures there cost more than a late launch.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your designer wants a custom date picker for a signature feel. Your engineer wants the platform's standard one. What do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Go with the designer,&quot; or &quot;pick the cheaper one.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs user value, consistency, accessibility, cost and maintenance, and tests.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that standard components carry accessibility and familiarity, proposes a compromise (custom only where it matters), sets a test and a decision rule, and keeps the relationship in view.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd not decide by rank. I'd frame the question around the user and the cost. What task does the date picker serve, and is it a differentiator (for example, a travel app where date selection is the core action) or a supporting control (a birthday in settings)? <em>For standard:</em> people already know it (consistency and standards), it comes with accessibility support (screen reader labels, keyboard and voice operation), it is cheaper to build and maintain, and it updates with the platform. <em>For custom:</em> it can make a core task faster or clearer, and it expresses the brand. Its costs: building and testing for accessibility, more bugs, and upkeep across devices. <em>How I'd settle it.</em> A quick test: prototype both and watch five users pick a date range for the core task. Measure time to complete, errors and preference. If the custom picker is clearly better at the core task, and the team commits to accessibility and maintenance, it is justified. If not, the standard wins. <em>A compromise.</em> Use standard components for supporting controls and invest in a custom one only where the task is central. I'd record the decision and the reason so it is not reopened without new evidence. I'd tell the designer and engineer that both views are right about something: one is about the user's task and the brand, the other about cost, accessibility and maintenance. What would change my mind: a usability result that contradicts my expectation.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> The growth team wants a pre-checked box that opts users into marketing emails. They expect opt-in to more than double. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do it, since it doubles opt-in,&quot; or &quot;refuse&quot; with no reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Applies an ethical test, mentions the legal and trust risk, and proposes an alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies net outcomes (engaged recipients, complaints), proposes a better-timed ask, sets guardrails and says how to take the conversation to the growth team.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start by taking the growth team's goal seriously: they want more people who receive and value marketing messages. Then I'd run the ethical nudge test (FW-C10-06). A pre-checked box is not transparent (many people do not notice it), is easy to leave only if you spot it, is not something users would endorse if they understood, and is not symmetric. It is a dark pattern. In several jurisdictions, consent rules require an affirmative action to opt in, so it may also be unlawful <code>[V: GDPR Article 4(11) (clear affirmative action) and the ePrivacy Directive (opt-in for marketing email), as summarized in guidance]</code>, and I'd ask legal and privacy teams immediately. <em>Count net outcomes.</em> Suppose an unchecked box gets 18% opt-in and a pre-checked box 41% (invented). The pre-checked list is bigger, but its members did not choose it: say 9% of them open the emails, against 31% of the people who chose. Engaged recipients per 100 sign-ups: 18 × 0.31 = 5.6 versus 41 × 0.09 = 3.7. The honest approach produces more engaged recipients. The pre-checked list also generates more unsubscribes and spam complaints, which damage the reputation of our sending domain and lower delivery to everyone. <em>An alternative that serves the goal.</em> Ask at a better moment, with a clear value: after the first order, &quot;Want order updates and occasional offers? Yes / No thanks,&quot; unchecked, with an easy way to change it later. Test the wording and timing. Measure engaged recipients per 100 sign-ups, complaints and unsubscribes, with opt-in rate as a diagnostic and not the target (<a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C05). <em>How I'd say it.</em> &quot;Opt-in is the wrong number. The right number is engaged subscribers, and the checkbox lowers it while raising legal risk. Let's test a better ask.&quot; What would change my mind: if the test showed that the honest ask gets too few opt-ins to matter, I'd look at the value we are offering, not at hiding the choice.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Leadership wants to skip accessibility work to make the launch date. What do you say?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Accessibility is the law&quot; or &quot;okay, we'll do it later.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains the users affected, the cost of retrofitting, and a minimum bar.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the audience, separates launch-blocking items from later improvements, notes legal exposure and brand risk, offers a dated plan and a scope trade.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a scoping conversation with data. First, who is affected: people who use screen readers, voice control, larger text, captions or switch devices, and, by the inclusive-design framing, people with temporary and situational limits (a broken arm, bright sun, a noisy room). Suppose we estimate that 8% of our users use assistive features or larger text (invented), which is 80,000 of a million. That is not an edge case. <em>Cost.</em> Retrofitting accessibility after launch usually costs far more than building it in, because it touches structure (labels, focus order, contrast, targets) that is expensive to change once shipped. Skipping it also creates risk: legal exposure in some markets <code>[V: consent and children's data rules differ by country (GDPR, COPPA, India's DPDP Act)]</code>, app-store and partner requirements, and brand damage when people who cannot use the product say so. <em>A minimum bar that does not threaten the date.</em> Launch-blocking items: labels for every control and image for screen readers, contrast of 4.5:1 for normal text (a designer can check in minutes), touch targets of adequate size, keyboard and voice operation of the core path, and no information carried by color alone. These are small if built in from the start. Defer to a dated plan: enhancements to advanced features, deeper testing with assistive-technology users, and nonessential motion refinements. <em>What I'd offer.</em> &quot;We can hold the date if we protect the core-path basics, which add about three days, and defer the advanced items to a dated plan with an owner. If we skip the basics we will spend more later and risk exclusion on day one.&quot; I'd bring a trade: cut a non-core feature before cutting accessibility. What would change my mind: if the launch is a small, internal pilot with no public users, I'd allow a later bar for the pilot, and I'd write down the date by which it must meet the standard.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c10-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C10-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C10 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a usability test, two of five people failed a critical task. The team says five is too small a sample to act on. How do you respond?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Five is enough,&quot; or &quot;we need more users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains what the test is for (finding problems) and what it cannot do (sizing them).</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives the discovery argument and the interval, separates the existence of a problem from its rate, proposes analytics and a cheap retest, and decides on cost.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The team is right about one thing and wrong about another. A five-person test cannot tell us how common the failure is: two of five is 40%, but the 95% interval for that is roughly 12% to 77%, so the true rate could be small or large. We should not report &quot;40% of users fail.&quot; But the purpose of the test is to find problems, not to count them. If each participant finds about 31% of the problems on average (the commonly reported figure <code>[V: Nielsen and Landauer (1993): one user finds about 31 percent of problems]</code>), five participants find about 84% of them (1 − 0.69⁵). Seeing two people fail a critical task, and seeing why, is strong evidence that a problem exists and what causes it. The question is not whether it exists; it does. The question is how much it matters and how cheaply we can fix it. <em>What I'd do.</em> First, look at the cause: if both failures came from the same visible issue (a hidden control, an unclear label), the fix is probably cheap, and I'd fix it without waiting. Second, size it with analytics: what share of live users reach that step and abandon, and how does it compare with other steps? Third, run another round with five new participants after the fix, since people who failed now know the answer. <em>Decision.</em> If the fix costs a day, make it now. If it costs a month, size the problem first with analytics and weigh it against other work. I'd tell the team: &quot;Five people cannot give us a rate, and they have shown us a real problem and its cause. Let's fix the cheap part and measure the rest.&quot; What would change my mind: if the two failures had different causes, or if analytics showed almost no one reaches that step.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is your favorite Apple product, and what would you change?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Praise (&quot;it just works, the design is beautiful&quot;) and a minor wish (&quot;make it cheaper&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the job, why the product does it better than alternatives, and one change with evidence and a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Picks a specific, modest product, backs the strength with observation from use, makes the change modest and tied to a segment, names the tradeoff and the risk, and claims nothing about the company's internal reasons.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(A composite candidate's answer; use your own product and experience.)</em> My pick is the wireless earbuds I use every day on my commute and for calls. The job: get audio into my ears with no effort, and switch between my phone and laptop without thinking about it. <em>Why it wins.</em> From my own use: pairing takes seconds when I open the case near my phone, and a spoken &quot;connected&quot; tells me it worked without looking at a screen. The case doubles as a charger, so I do not manage a cable. Those are strengths I'd protect: anything that adds a step to connecting would hurt the whole experience. <em>What I'd change.</em> One thing I observe: it is hard to know the case's battery level at a glance when the earbuds are in the case and the lid is closed, and I have been caught out on a long trip. I'd guess many commuters feel that, though I can't know how many. The problem: &quot;A commuter cannot tell whether the case has enough charge before leaving home, so they sometimes run out mid-trip.&quot; <em>Recommendation.</em> A small <em>add</em>: a battery reminder on the phone the night before, if the case is low and I have a regular early commute, which the phone could infer from my routine. Keep it opt-in, since an unwanted notification is a cost. Metric: the share of users who see a low-battery event during their commute, with a guardrail on how many switch the reminder off. Risk: noise, and use of routine data, which should stay on the device. <em>Tradeoff.</em> A reminder helps people with predictable routines and annoys the rest, so I'd make it a suggestion and test it with a small group first. What would change my mind: if few people actually run out of charge, the problem is not worth a feature. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that &quot;favorite product&quot; questions are common at Apple and reward specific, detail-aware answers <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product's behavior or the company's internal decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Pick the app you used most today and critique it.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's great; I'd add more features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the goal and user, walks the flow, names strengths, ranks issues and recommends a change with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses observation from today's use as evidence, separates observation from inference, notes the strategic position briefly, and says what would change the view.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(Composite.)</em> The app I used most is a messaging app. I'll judge it as a way for me to stay in touch with a handful of people and a few busy group chats with little effort. I'll critique for a person like me: many groups, some of them active. <em>Flow.</em> Open, see the list of chats, open one, read, reply, return. <em>Strengths:</em> replying is fast, and message status (sent, read) is clear. I'd protect both. <em>Ranked issues (from today's use).</em> (1) Two group chats produced 80 messages while I was in meetings, and I could not tell which contained something addressed to me; I scrolled for several minutes (severity 4, reach high among people in busy groups). (2) Search found the message but opened the chat at the bottom, not at the message, so I scrolled to find the context (3). (3) Notification settings are per chat and buried (2). I'd rank the first at the top because it is the reason people mute groups, which weakens the product's core value. <em>Observation versus inference.</em> I observed the 80 messages and the scrolling. I infer that many people with busy groups feel this, and that muting is the response. I can't know the numbers. <em>Recommendation.</em> The problem: &quot;After time away, a member of a busy group cannot find what matters to them.&quot; A catch-up view that shows mentions and replies to me first, optional and in addition to the normal chat. Metric: the share of members of busy groups still active in the group after eight weeks, with a guardrail on messages sent per member (so the feature does not discourage posting). Risk: it reduces the visibility of messages not addressed to anyone, so I'd keep the full view as the default. Strategically, a messaging product depends on groups staying alive, which is why this issue matters to the business and not only to me. <em>Close.</em> &quot;It does quick replies well; its biggest problem for people in busy groups is finding what matters after time away. I'd add an optional catch-up view and measure retention in busy groups. I'd check it with five users and with the mute rate.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Critique the pairing and setup experience of wireless earbuds.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Make pairing easier and add more colors.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats it as a first-run experience across hardware and software, ranks issues, and recommends fixes with metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates what can be changed after shipping from what cannot, lets accessibility override the score, uses proxies for lagging outcomes such as returns, and avoids claims about any real product.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the category from use and from public information; nothing here describes a specific product. <em>Purpose and users.</em> The earbuds should take a new owner from the box to listening in about a minute and keep them listening. I'll critique for a first-time owner with a phone they already own, and note switchers as a second segment. The first-run experience is the first impression and the point where returns begin. <em>Flow.</em> Open the box, power on, pair, set up optional features (fit test, controls, assistant, companion app), first listening session. For a device there are extra surfaces: indicator lights, sounds and what the case shows. <em>Strengths.</em> Pairing that begins when the case opens near the phone (a pattern several makers use) is nearly effortless; a spoken &quot;connected&quot; gives eyes-free feedback. Protect both. <em>Ranked issues.</em> | Issue | Severity | Reach | Impact | Fixability | |---|---|---|---|---| | The fit test, which affects sound quality, sits deep in the app | 3 | 5 | 15 | 5 | | A failed pairing shows only a blinking light, with no message | 5 | 2 | 10 | 4 | | Connecting a second device is not obvious | 3 | 3 | 9 | 2 | | Case light colors are unexplained | 2 | 4 | 8 | 5 | | Setup guidance is visual only | 5 | 1 | 5 | 3 | By score, the fit test is first. The last row is an accessibility barrier: a low-vision owner cannot complete setup without help, so it is a requirement and goes on the list whatever its reach. <em>Hardware and software.</em> Some fixes live in the app and can be shipped any time (a fit-test prompt, a failure message, spoken guidance); others live in the device's firmware or hardware (light behavior, sounds) and may be fixed only before manufacture or by an update if the device supports it. I'd separate the lists and say which is which. <em>Recommendation.</em> The problem: &quot;A first-time owner does not find the fit test and gets no guidance when pairing fails, so some have poor sound and some return the product.&quot; Moves: an app prompt that offers the fit test right after connection (skippable), and a clear failure message with &quot;try again / reset&quot; plus a distinct sound. Metrics: primary, the share of new owners who reach a first listening session within five minutes; input, the share who complete the fit test; guardrails: pairing success, support contacts and returns in the first month; counter metric: the share who skip setup entirely. Returns arrive weeks later, so I'd use the five-minute measure as a proxy and check that it predicts fewer returns. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that critique prompts at Apple include device first-run experiences <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Critique a food-delivery app, from both the customer's and the business's point of view.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Fees are too high; add loyalty rewards.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Critiques the customer's flow and the business's economics, and notes where they conflict.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the multi-sided tension (customers, restaurants, couriers), ranks issues for one segment, applies the strategic lens with numbers labeled as assumptions, and recommends with a guardrail on the other sides.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Customer view.</em> The goal: a household gets a hot meal on a weeknight with little effort and little uncertainty. The flow: choose a restaurant, build an order, pay, wait, receive. Strengths: browsing is easy and reordering a past meal is quick, which I'd protect. Ranked issues: (1) uncertainty during the wait: the estimate shifts and there is little explanation when it does (severity 4, reach high); (2) substitutions and missing items, handled after the fact (4, medium); (3) the total cost is clear only at the last step, where fees appear (3, high). I'd rank the first at the top because uncertainty is anxiety, and it drives support contacts. <em>Business view.</em> A delivery marketplace has three sides: customers, restaurants and couriers, and its economics depend on all of them. Suppose (invented) an average order of $30, a 25% commission, and $7 of courier and handling cost per order, leaving $0.50 contribution before marketing and support. That is thin, so each support contact (say $4) or discount matters. Distribution relies on promotions and advertising (inferred), and defensibility is limited because customers can use several apps. This explains why the company is sensitive to wait-time contacts and to fee transparency: both affect contact cost and conversion. <em>Where views conflict.</em> Showing the total cost early could lower conversion (people see the fee sooner) while raising satisfaction and reducing refunds. The honest design is to show it early, and measure net outcomes. <em>Recommendation.</em> Fix the wait: show an honest estimate with a range and a reason when it changes. Metric: delivery-related support contacts per 100 orders, with guardrails on conversion at checkout and on courier earnings (a more accurate estimate should not be achieved by pressuring couriers), and a counter metric on the share of customers who cancel after seeing a longer estimate. I'd label my numbers as assumptions and my view of the economics as inference.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c11-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C11-05</h3>\n";
